<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Malawi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lake Nyasa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>, also called Lake Nyasa, is a long lake in the southern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">East African Rift</a>, bordered by Malawi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and Tanzania. Its shores were home to the Maravi states and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngoni">Ngoni</a>, Yao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> rulers who traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves to the coast. Missions and British rule from the 1880s led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of Nyasaland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-malawi.jpg" alt="Lake Malawi" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Malawi_seen_from_orbit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> is between 560 and 580 kilometres long and about 75 kilometres wide at its widest point, with a surface area of about 29,600 square kilometres. Its deepest point, in a depression in the north-central part, is 706 metres, while the southern half is shallower. The Shire River drains it at the south into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a>. It is a meromictic lake, which means its water layers do not mix.</p><p>The lake lies in a valley formed by the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">East African Rift</a>, where the African plate is being split. It was long estimated to be 1 to 2 million years old, but more recent evidence points to a considerably older lake, whose basin began to form about 8.6 million years ago. Its levels have varied dramatically, from almost 600 metres below the present level to 10 to 20 metres above it, and at times the lake nearly dried out. Between 1390 and 1860 it may have stood 120 to 150 metres below its present level.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Portuguese trader Candido José da Costa Cardoso was the first European to visit the lake, in 1846. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> reached it in 1859 and named it Lake Nyasa. He also called it the Lake of Stars, after the lights of fishermen's lanterns on the water, and the Lake of Storms, after the violent gales that sweep it.</p><p>On 16 August 1914 the lake saw a brief naval battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The British gunboat Gwendolen found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>'s only gunboat on the lake, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-von-wissmann">Hermann von Wissmann</a>, in a bay near Sphinxhaven and disabled it with a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> shot from about 1,800 metres. The Times hailed it as the first naval victory of the war for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><p>The lake's borders rest on colonial agreements. In 1954 a British and Portuguese agreement made the middle of the lake the boundary with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, except for Chizumulu and Likoma islands, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>. Between Malawi and Tanzania both sides cite the Heligoland Treaty of 1890, and Tanzania protested officially in 1967.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The lake is known as Lake Malawi, Lake Nyasa in Tanzania and Lago Niassa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. It is home to more species of fish than any other lake, including at least 700 species of cichlid. Part of it is included in Lake Malawi National Park at the southern end, and the portion in Mozambique was declared a reserve in 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Malawi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scottish missions at Blantyre and Livingstonia and the African Lakes Company drew Britain into the <b>Lake Malawi</b> region.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-central-africa-protectorate">British Central Africa Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>Tumbuka tradition recalls that in the late 18th century traders known as the Balowoka crossed <b>Lake Malawi</b> from the east seeking ivory.</blockquote><a href="/state/chikulamayembe-state">Chikulamayembe State</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1845 Salim bin Abdallah, a Swahili-Arab trader from the coast connected with Zanzibar, settled at Nkhotakota on the western shore of <b>Lake Malawi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/jumbe-state-of-nkhotakota">Jumbe State of Nkhotakota</a></li><li><blockquote>According to tradition, the Phiri clan migrated from the Luba lands of the Congo basin and settled south-west of <b>Lake Malawi</b> in the late 15th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-maravi">Kingdom of Maravi</a></li><li><blockquote>The Makanjira chiefdom was founded by Yao who moved to the southeastern shore of <b>Lake Malawi</b> in the mid-19th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/makanjira-chiefdom">Makanjira Chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>They moved as far north as the Songea area of southern Tanzania under Mputa before being driven south again, settling around 1870 in the highlands southwest of <b>Lake Malawi</b> under Chidiaonga.</blockquote><a href="/state/maseko-ngoni-kingdom">Maseko Ngoni Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
